{%- assign url_parts = page.permalink | split: '/' -%}
{%- assign url_parts_size = url_parts | size -%}
{%- assign parent_permalink = "" -%}
<nav class="main-menu">
  <span class="main-menu__breadcrumbs">
    {%- comment -%}
    Always include the "Hem" part of breadcrumbs.

    The inlne if-statement, that also exists in the other <a> tag below,
    is there to set the active class on the manu if we are on the page the link represents.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a class="main-menu__link main-menu__link--breadcrumb{% if page.permalink == '/home' %} main-menu__link--active{% endif %}" href="/home">Hem</a>
    {%- comment -%}
    Skip the /home page, since it is standing in for / in our menu structure.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- unless page.permalink == '/home' -%}
      {%- comment -%}
      The permalink starts with a /, '/hosting' for example, and when we split it we get ['','hosting'],
      so we need to skip the first one since it is always just an empty string.

      Loop over the url_parts of the current page, for example ['','hosting','seo','details'],
      and for each new step build a string containing all the parts until now and link to that page.
      So we already have a link to /home from above, now we add links to /hosting, /hosting/seo and
      /hosting/seo/details, linking the pages with their title as text so we end up with these breadcrumbs:
      Hem / Hemsida / Sökmotoroptimering / Detaljer
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- for url_part in url_parts offset:1 -%}
        {%- assign parent_permalink = parent_permalink | append: '/' | append: url_part -%}
        {%- assign parent_page = site.pages | where:"permalink", parent_permalink  | first -%}
        {%- if parent_page -%}
          {%- comment -%}Normal pages{%- endcomment -%}
          <a class="main-menu__link main-menu__link--breadcrumb{% if page.permalink == parent_page.permalink %} main-menu__link--active{% endif %}" href="{{ parent_page.permalink }}">{{ parent_page.title }}</a>
        {%- else -%}
          {%- comment -%}Specifically for blog pages where the actual path to the page doesn't match the url path{%- endcomment -%}
          <a class="main-menu__link main-menu__link--breadcrumb main-menu__link--active" href="{{ permalink }}">{{ page.title }}</a>
          {% break %}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endunless -%}
  </span>
  {%- comment -%}
  On to the real menu ... First we want to find all the pages that are children of the current page, skipping /home as usual.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign search = "page.permalink contains 'TERM'"  | replace: "TERM", parent_permalink  -%}
  {%- assign child_pages = site.pages | where_exp:"page", search | where_exp:"page", "page.permalink != '/home'" | where_exp:"page", "page.hidden != true" | sort: 'order' -%}
  {%- comment -%}
  Loop through all the pages we found and if we are on /home we want all the pages that are on the same level,
  without a parent page - like /hosting, /training etc, as opposed to /hosting/seo that has the parent /hosting - and if
  we are not on /home we want all the pages that are just one step up in the hierarchy. Since we have already filtered
  on the parent permalink, like /hosting, we will only get direct children of /hosting this way, like /hosting/seo.

  We do this by counting the parts of the url, how many / are in the url basically. For /home we want all pages whos
  permalink has the same number of parts as /home. For any other page we want the pages thet has one more part to their
  permalink.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% for node in child_pages %}
    {% assign node_url_parts = node.permalink | split: '/' %}
    {% if page.permalink == '/home' %}
      {% assign node_url_parts_size = node_url_parts | size %}
    {% else %}
      {% assign node_url_parts_size = node_url_parts | size | minus: 1 %}
    {% endif %}
    {% if url_parts_size == node_url_parts_size %}
      <a class="main-menu__link{% if node.permalink == page.permalink %} main-menu__link--active{% endif %}" href='{{node.permalink}}'>{{node.title}}</a>
    {% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
</nav>
